 2. 🗳️ Dems&#x27; unlikely plan: Make Trump look good 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Photo: Alex Wong/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Holly Otterbein — co-author of our weekly Axios 2028 newsletter — writes from the What's Next summit, a gathering of centrist Dems in Denver: 

 Democrats' euphoria over their once-in-a-generation wave of midterm victories is giving way to a reality check:

 They were lucky to have a weakened opposition. And they might be "renting" this year's voters if they don't quickly deliver on their promises. Why it matters: If Democrats want to rack up policy wins ASAP, they'll need to work with President Trump — the guy many of them held up as what's wrong with America. That means giving him a few wins.

 🔬 Zoom in: Texas Sen.-elect James Talarico argued exactly that late last week at the What's Next summit.

 "If President Trump wants the same plaques and portraits and busts as LBJ, we can help make that a reality," Talarico said. "We should make the final two years of his presidency the most productive of any presidency in American history." Gina Hinojosa, who'll be Texas' first Democratic governor in more than three decades, has said she'll work with Trump on immigration . Michigan Sen.-elect Abdul El-Sayed, a frequent Trump target, is pitching the president on rebranding Medicare for All as " TrumpCare " to get it passed. On CNN's "State of the Union" yesterday, Mississippi Sen.-elect Scott Colom told Dana Bash he wants to collaborate with Trump to deliver on the president's own past promise to lower grocery prices. 🔭 Zoom out: Some top Democrats are determined not to repeat the mistakes the party made after the 2022 elections, when they overperformed — and grew complacent. They lost the presidential election two years later.

