 6. 🏛️ Administration threatens to prosecute Dems 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Via X 

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 White House Chief of Staff Stephen Miller tweeted support yesterday after a federal prosecutor accused Sen. Elizabeth Warren (D-Mass.), incoming chair of the Senate Banking Committee, of intending to "weaponize congressional investigations."

 Why it matters: The Trump administration, with a series of such letters to House and Senate Democrats , is preemptively warning Democrats, seven weeks before they take over the House and Senate, that the Justice Department could consider overly aggressive "congressional investigations and subpoenas" to be a crime. The prosecutor, U.S. Attorney Aaron Reitz of Houston, ordered Warren to preserve "all potentially relevant evidence related to your efforts to suppress associational activities related to President Trump and the Trump Administration," for potential use in a conspiracy case .

 "Failure to do so ... may carry significant legal consequences," Reitz said. Reitz argued in a two-page letter to Warren that her comments in post-midterm interviews suggest she will "target private individuals close to President Trump." Reitz added: "The American people are entitled to freely associate without their elected officials terrorizing them."

 Reitz told The Wall Street Journal : "We don't have to wait for them to commit a crime for us to begin investigating whether there's a basis to escalate or indict or prosecute ... [Democrats] are champing at the bit with bloodlust to punish those who associate with the president." Reality check: The Constitution grants lawmakers legal immunity for legislative actions.

 
 
